---
import DownloadPicker from "../components/DownloadPicker.svelte";
import { BASE } from "../base";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { DESKTOP_BUILDS, EXTENSIONS, RELEASES, REPO_SLUG, USERSCRIPT, downloadUrl } from "../site";

const builds = DESKTOP_BUILDS.map((build) => ({ ...build, url: downloadUrl(build.file) }));
---

<Page
  title="Download"
  description="Parousia Desktop for Windows and Linux, the browser extension for Chromium and Firefox, and the userscript."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="prose">
    <h1>Download</h1>
    <p>
      You need Parousia Desktop and one way to reach it from a browser: the extension, or the
      userscript. Every file below is the newest <a href={RELEASES}>release</a>.
      <a href={`${BASE}docs/installation/`}>Installation</a> walks through the first run.
    </p>

    <h2>Parousia Desktop</h2>
    <DownloadPicker client:load builds={builds} />
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            builds.map((build) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{build.label}</td>
                <td><a href={build.url}>{build.file}</a></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Each is a single file with nothing to install. The Linux builds are static, so they run on any
      distribution; make the file executable first (<code>chmod +x</code>). There is no macOS build
      yet.
    </p>

    <h2>Browser extension</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Browser</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            EXTENSIONS.map((extension) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{extension.label}</td>
                <td><a href={downloadUrl(extension.file)}>{extension.file}</a></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Store listings are not up yet, so for now the extension loads from the zip; see
      <a href={`${BASE}docs/installation/#the-browser-extension`}>Installation</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Userscript</h2>
    <p>
      For browsers where the extension will not go, and for a script manager you already use:
      <a href={downloadUrl(USERSCRIPT.file)}>{USERSCRIPT.file}</a> ({USERSCRIPT.label}). Desktop
      accepts a userscript only after you turn on <em>Allow userscripts</em>, because that lets any
      web page reach it.
    </p>

    <h2>Check what you downloaded</h2>
    <p>
      Every release has a <code>SHA256SUMS</code> file. Put it next to your downloads and run:
    </p>
    <pre><code>sha256sum --ignore-missing -c SHA256SUMS</code></pre>
    <p>On Windows, <code>(Get-FileHash file).Hash</code> in PowerShell gives the value to compare. Each file also has a build provenance attestation, which shows the workflow run that built it:</p>
    <pre><code>{`gh attestation verify parousia-desktop-linux-x86_64 --repo ${REPO_SLUG}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</Page>
